    <bf:title>
      <bf:Title>
	<bf:mainTitle>Financing poverty : consumption and debt dynamics in racialized São Paulo</bf:mainTitle>
      </bf:Title>
    </bf:title>
    <bf:content>
      <bf:Content rdf:about="http://id.loc.gov/vocabulary/contentTypes/txt">
	<rdfs:label>text</rdfs:label>
	<bf:code>txt</bf:code>
      </bf:Content>
    </bf:content>
    <bf:tableOfContents>
      <bf:TableOfContents>
	<rdfs:label>Introduction -- São Paulo : a city of superlatives -- Housing, labor, and wage in São Paulo's periphery -- Between needs and desires : scarcity and unsatisfied basic needs in Brasilândia, Jardim Ângela and Jardim Helene -- Contrasts under the same roof : consumer durables in black households in São Paulo's periphery -- The first payment is in 30 days : the spread of credit to lower-income population -- "They may be late, but they pay" : default, insolvency, and the mathematics of everyday life -- Conclusion : financing poverty.</rdfs:label>
      </bf:TableOfContents>
    </bf:tableOfContents>
    <bf:summary>
      <bf:Summary>
	<rdfs:label>"This book examines how credit expansion has reshaped everyday life for Black residents of São Paulo's peripheries, where consumer goods coexist with infrastructural precarity. Drawing on more than a decade of research and 150 interviews, Kauê Lopes dos Santos shows how low-income populations have been incorporated into financial circuits through installment plans, retail credit, and digital platforms. Rather than signaling social mobility, this process produces new forms of indebtedness and vulnerability, embedding financial logics in domestic life. Centering race as a constitutive dimension of urban inequality, Lopes dos Santos argues that the financialization of poverty reconfigures--rather than reduces--inequality. Bridging urban geography and Black geographies, Financing Poverty offers a powerful account of how capital penetrates the margins of the city and reorganizes everyday survival"-- Provided by publisher.</rdfs:label>
      </bf:Summary>
    </bf:summary>
    <dcterms:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://id.loc.gov/resources/works"/>
